.TH mbpy_xyplot 1 "13 September 2026" "MB-System 5.0" "MB-System 5.0"
.SH NAME
\fBmbpy_xyplot\fP \- Create an executable shellscript which will generate
a GMT modern-mode plot of xy data from one or more data files.

.SH VERSION
Version 5.0

.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBmbpy_xyplot\fP \fB\-I\fP\fIfile\fP[\fB:\fP\fIkey=value...\fP]
[\fB-I\fP\fIfile\fP[\fB:\fP\fIkey=value...\fP] ...]
[\fB\-O\fP\fIroot\fP]
[\fB\-\-format\fP\fIfmt\fP]
[\fB\-\-title\fP\fItext\fP]
[\fB\-\-xlabel\fP\fItext\fP]
[\fB\-\-ylabel\fP\fItext\fP]
[\fB\-\-dpi\fP\fIdpi\fP]
[\fB\-X\fP] [\fB\-V\fP]
.br
[\fB\-R\fP\fIxmin/xmax/ymin/ymax\fP[\fIr\fP]]
[\fB\-J\fP\fIspec\fP]
[\fB\-P\fP\fIpagesize\fP]
[\fB\-\-portrait\fP | \fB\-\-landscape\fP]
[\fB\-B\fP\fIspec\fP]
[\fB\-\-length\-scale\fP\fIspec\fP]
.br
[\fB\-S\fP\fIsymbol\fP]
[\fB\-G\fP\fIcolor\fP]
[\fB\-W\fP\fIpen\fP]
.br
[\fB\-\-coast\fP]
[\fB\-\-coast\-resolution\fP\fI{f,h,i,l,c}\fP]
[\fB\-\-coast\-dry\-fill\fP\fIcolor\fP]
[\fB\-\-coast\-wet\-fill\fP\fIcolor\fP]
[\fB\-\-coast\-lake\-fill\fP\fIcolor\fP]
[\fB\-\-coast\-river\fP\fIcolor\fP]
[\fB\-\-coast\-boundary\fP\fIspec\fP]
[\fB\-\-coast\-pen\fP\fIpen\fP]
.br
[\fB\-\-text\fP\fIlon/lat/size/angle/font/justify/text\fP]
.br
[\fB\-\-gmt\-default\fP\fIparam/value\fP]

.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBmbpy_xyplot\fP is a Python/PyGMT-era replacement for the Perl macro
\fBmbm_xyplot\fP. It reads one or more xy data files and generates an
executable shellscript of \fBGMT\fP \fBmodern mode\fP commands (\fBgmt
begin\fP / \fBgmt end\fP) which, when run, plots them as lines,
symbols, or both, optionally overlaid with a coastline and/or text
labels. Axes may be an auto-fit linear (Cartesian) scale or any GMT map
projection.

The plot is scaled to fit the specified page size using the same
page-layout, projection-fitting, and tick-interval heuristics as
\fBmbm_histplot\fP -- confirmed by direct comparison of the two Perl
macros' own sources to be identical (aside from whitespace), and
distinct from \fBmbm_grdplot\fP's own versions of the same
computations (see \fBmbpy_histplot\fP(1)). In particular, when no
\fB\-\-projection\fP is given, \fBmbpy_xyplot\fP computes two
\fIindependent\fP x and y scales that each separately stretch to fill
the page (unlike \fBmbpy_grdplot\fP/\fBmbpy_grd3dplot\fP's single
aspect-ratio-preserving scale, which would be wrong here since an xy
plot's x and y axes routinely have unrelated units and ranges -- e.g. a
time-series plot).

Unlike \fBmbm_xyplot\fP's single-letter \fB\-M\fP\fIsub\fP\fB:\fP\fIsub\fP\fB:\fP...
grammar for coastline/text-label/length-scale tuning, \fBmbpy_xyplot\fP
exposes each of those as its own descriptive long option (see OPTIONS
below).

\fBmbpy_xyplot\fP never writes a persistent temporary file, unlike
\fBmbm_xyplot\fP itself: for every input file, \fBmbm_xyplot\fP
preprocesses (delimiter-splits and, if requested, applies a column-
selection/math expression to) the data into a file named
\fI<input><n>.<pid>\fP left behind in the current directory after every
run, whether or not the plot script is ever executed. \fBmbpy_xyplot\fP
instead performs the equivalent column-selection/math once in memory,
in Python, to determine the auto-fit plot bounds, and at plot time
pipes each input file through a small \fBawk\fP(1) program straight
into \fBgmt plot\fP's standard input -- a live, ephemeral pipe, with
nothing written to disk.

Each \fB\-\-input\fP file may carry its own colon-separated overrides,
in \fIkey\fP\fB=\fP\fIvalue\fP form:
\fBsymbol=\fP, \fBfill=\fP, \fBpen=\fP (styling, defaulting from
\fB\-S\fP/\fB\-G\fP/\fB\-W\fP), \fBdelimiter=\fP (default: whitespace),
and \fBxexpr=\fP/\fByexpr=\fP (column-selection/math expressions using
\fBmbm_xyplot\fP's own \fBc[\fP\fIN\fP\fB]\fP field-selection and
\fB#\fP line-number mini-language, e.g. \fBc[3]\fP or \fBc[3]*2+1\fP;
default \fBxexpr=c[1]\fP, \fByexpr=c[2]\fP). A line beginning with
\fB>\fP is always passed through unchanged as a multi-segment marker,
matching \fBmbm_xyplot\fP's (and \fBgmt plot\fP's) own convention.

A genuine bug was found and fixed while validating this port against
real-world data: \fBmbm_xyplot\fP's own per-line numeric-result check
is unintentionally a no-op (its validation regex is satisfied by the
empty string), so an ordinary text header line at the top of a data
file passes through into the plotted file unfiltered -- and because
\fBgmt plot\fP infers each column's data type from the \fIfirst\fP
record it reads, a single such non-numeric first line makes it silently
discard every subsequent, perfectly valid row instead of just that one
line. \fBmbpy_xyplot\fP validates each computed x/y pair with a real
numeric check instead, skipping (with a warning) only the genuinely
bad lines.

A related, deliberate addition with no equivalent in \fBmbm_xyplot\fP
at all: a data column that is exactly constant (e.g. a real "heave"
trace logged as 0.0 for an entire survey) collapses to a zero-width
axis that neither classic nor modern GMT can project. \fBmbpy_xyplot\fP
pads such a degenerate axis by a small margin so it still plots as a
visible flat line instead of aborting.

\fBmbpy_xyplot\fP requires \fBGMT\fP 6 or later (modern mode), a POSIX
\fBawk\fP(1), and a Python 3 interpreter. It depends on
\fBmbpy_layout.py\fP and \fBbaseline_gmt_defaults()\fP from
\fBmbpy_grdplot_script.py\fP (shared with \fBmbpy_grdplot\fP/
\fBmbpy_grd3dplot\fP), and reuses the page-size/tick-interval helper
functions from \fBmbpy_histplot_script.py\fP, plus its own
\fBmbpy_xyplot_script.py\fP, all installed alongside it.

